<resources>
    <!-- Minimal pre-Compose theme — MainActivity's content is 100% Compose
         (see ui/theme/Theme.kt for the real design system). This only needs
         to set the window background so there's no flash before the first
         Compose frame lands.

         Two variants, not one: the app's theme is a persisted user toggle
         (not system light/dark), so MainActivity picks between these via
         `setTheme()` using a value read synchronously from DataStore before
         `super.onCreate()` — see MainActivity's `initialDarkTheme`. Without
         a light variant, a light-theme user's cold launch painted this dark
         windowBackground first, then had the entire Compose UI pop from
         dark to light once the (async) theme preference finished loading —
         a whole-UI color flash on every launch that dark-theme users never
         saw, since their default already matched. -->
    <style name="Theme.PearTvLauncher" parent="android:Theme.Material.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/peartv_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowFullscreen">true</item>
    </style>

    <style name="Theme.PearTvLauncher.Light" parent="Theme.PearTvLauncher">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/peartv_background_light</item>
    </style>

    <!-- MainActivity's manifest-declared theme (AndroidManifest.xml) — this,
         not Theme.PearTvLauncher above, is what's actually on screen at cold
         process start, before any of MainActivity's Kotlin runs at all. API
         30 has no native SplashScreen (that's API 31+), so this project runs
         entirely on androidx.core:core-splashscreen's own compat renderer,
         which reads these windowSplashScreen* attributes off whatever theme
         is active the moment installSplashScreen() is called in onCreate —
         these are load-bearing on every device this ships to, not a
         progressive-enhancement nicety for newer OSes only.

         A single fixed icon/background regardless of the user's light/dark
         preference: that preference is a DataStore read MainActivity doesn't
         resolve until partway through onCreate, well after this theme has
         already been on screen for at least one frame — there's no signal
         to branch on this early. postSplashScreenTheme is the *fallback*
         MainActivity's real content theme reverts to once the splash exits,
         only if MainActivity's own conditional `setTheme()` call (see its
         own doc) didn't already override it first — which it always does in
         practice, so this is a safety net, not the actual light/dark switch. -->
    <style name="Theme.PearTvLauncher.Starting" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/peartv_background</item>
        <!-- Not app_logo_grey.png directly — confirmed on-device (physical
             Shield) that the SplashScreen framework (native API 31+, and
             this compat library replicating it on our actual API 30 floor)
             renders windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon inside a fixed circular
             "icon disc" by default, same convention as adaptive-icon
             masking. app_logo_grey.png is a full-bleed square badge (own
             background + "PearTV" wordmark reaching near its edges) — a
             circular mask clips its corners and slices through the
             wordmark. splash_icon.png is the same badge, pixel-identical,
             just re-padded onto a transparent canvas at ~60% scale (see the
             script that generated it, kept in this commit's history) so
             its own corners stay inside the circle with margin to spare —
             not a from-scratch redesign, and not a glyph-only cutout either
             (the source art's glyph/background boundary is a soft
             emboss gradient, not a hard edge, so a clean isolated cutout
             isn't reliably extractable from it — see project memory on
             this if revisiting). -->
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/splash_icon</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/Theme.PearTvLauncher</item>
    </style>
</resources>
